# Refuter report on paper3/latex/main.pdf (62 pages, built 2026-11-05 07:50 WET)

Written to `/tmp/paper3_refuter/REPORT.md`; the same text follows.

**Summary.** I read the PDF text in full and checked every citable claim against the clone at 4eaee875, the manuscript extract, the two audits, the replay logs, the fetched related-work records, the companion Lean files and logs, the practitioner data files, and my own arithmetic. The paper's central numbers (every value of rho(n)/n, the peak, the crossing at 50, the return below 1 near e^5504 = 10^2390.36 with 2,391 digits, the clause-count bound 2,604,376, the constants 256+At / C+28700 / A+1 / S+5022741 / d+2 / e+2, the cutoff, every comparator line number, every manuscript page/line but one, every replay and nanoda number, every related-work quotation, the Lean companion claims, and all of Table 7.1) agree with the sources. Findings: **A: 6** (one false sentence about where "ETH" occurs in the Lean, one wrong manuscript page, a line-count artefact of +1 per file, a 41-vs-42 dependency count, two imprecise line citations); **B: 4**; **C: 1**; **D: 0**; **E: 0**; **F: 5**; **G: 5**. No finding touches the paper's stance or its verdict on the proof.

Counting rule used for manuscript pages: `page(L) = 1 + (number of lines before line L that contain a form feed)`; form feeds sit at the start of lines 53, 104, 155, 208, 260, ... (67 in all, 3,815 lines). Under the alternative rule (count form feeds in lines up to and including L) every cited page comes out the same, including the one disagreement below.

---

## A — factual disagreements, wrong line/page numbers, wrong numbers

**R1 (A).** p. 16, §3.5: *"The hypotheses themselves are not defined in the library (the strings "ETH" and "SETH" occur in the collection's Lean only in two files of a different family, lean/OAI/Combinatorics/ParityLifts/, ...)."* Checked with `grep -rnw ETH` over `lean/**/*.lean`. "ETH" occurs, besides the two ParityLifts files, in **six files of this family**, all inside comments: `SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean:6` ("the unchanged ETH square-root-log time scale"), `SqrtLogSpace/NativeOuterBudgets.lean:147`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeQueryTimeBounds.lean:49`, `SqrtLogSpace/BooleanDifferences.lean:7` and `:228`, `SqrtLogSpace/NativeHashQueries.lean:377`; and the theorem name `exact_count_eth` (Final.lean:104) contains it in lower case. "SETH" indeed occurs nowhere as a word. The substantive point (no hypothesis is defined in the library) stands; the sentence as written is false.

**R2 (A).** p. 48, §9.6: *"In the manuscript, "practical" occurs once (page 4, line 153) ..."*. Line 153 of the extract ("Experiments on formulas from circuit synthesis and diagnosis demonstrated the practical value of") is on **page 3**: form feeds precede it at lines 53 and 104 only; page 4 begins at line 155. The count "once" and the eleven occurrences of "exhaustive" (lines 170, 311, 395, 411, 705, 2210, 2324, 2480, 2487, 2509, 3804) agree.

**R3 (A).** p. 20, §4.2 and Table 4.1 p. 22: *"... totalling 550,551 lines; the largest file is 2,873 lines (SqrtLogSpace/DisjointReuse/SparsePriorityProgram.lean)."* Recounted with `wc -l` and Python `splitlines()` over the same 3,091 modules (my own import-closure script reproduces the 3,091 names, 50 Mathlib modules, 1 core module and 886 outside files exactly): **547,460 lines and 2,872 lines**. The paper's figures are exactly 3,091 more in total and 1 more for the file, i.e. the audit's `closure.py` counts `len(text.split('\n'))`, one more than `wc -l` for every file ending in a newline. `proof_audit.md` carries the same 550,551 and 2,873. State the convention or use the `wc -l` figures.

**R4 (A).** p. 26, §5.1: *"... (its lakefile) clones its 41 dependencies at build time"*. p. 23, §4.6 says *"The manifest ... lists 42 packages"* and Table 4.1 says 42. `lake-manifest.json` has 42 packages and the replay copy's `.lake/packages/` holds 42 directories (mathlib included); nothing is 41. The "41" comes from `paper3/replay/README.md` ("41 packages under .lake/packages"), wrong by the same one. Internal inconsistency 41 vs 42.

**R5 (A).** p. 7, §2.3: *"... a program that uses 80 one-bit memory cells ("registers") and 1,220 tapes (SqrtLogSpace/Final.lean, lines 98–101)"*, and p. 58, B.2 likewise. Lines 98–101 of Final.lean contain `1220`, `256` and `1216` but not `80`; the `80` is in `BitProgram.Program 80 1220 labels` at line 11 (also 105, 150). B.3 (p. 59) cites line 11 correctly; §2.3 and B.2 do not.

**R6 (A).** p. 59, B.3: *"GuardedCompiler.primitive_execution (SqrtLogSpace/GuardedCompiler.lean, lines 158–162) ..."*. `primitive_execution` is declared at **lines 160–162**; line 158 is the last line of `compiler_overhead`'s proof. §4.5's "lines 152–162" (spanning both lemmas) is fine.

## B — numbers or quotations with no stated source or regenerate command

**R7 (B).** p. 6, §2.2 (and p. 56, B.1): *"A formula with 125 variables, 500 clauses and width 4 becomes 1,006,223 bits; one with 1,000 variables, 4,000 clauses and width 3 becomes 47.9 million bits."* The encoded length depends on which literals the formula contains (unary codes `2·var+sign`), not on (n, m, k) alone: emulating the comparator's definitions on five random width-4 formulas with n = 125, m = 500 gives 990,775; 1,013,703; 1,005,403; 1,017,823; 1,021,583 bits. `check_encoding.py` is named but the formula/seed giving 1,006,223 is not, so the sentence reads as if the three parameters determined the number. Say "one random formula with ..." and name the seed, or quote only the bound 2,006,127 (which I reproduce).

**R8 (B).** p. 26, §5.1: *"... the collection's default target, which would build all 721 families"*. No source or regenerate command. Cheap recounts give none of 721: `lean/OAI.lean` has 928 import lines, `lean/ComparatorChallenges/` has 418 JSONs and 418 `.lean` files, `lean/docs/` has 245 files. The replay README states 721 without derivation.

**R9 (B).** p. 36, §7.1: *"... counting those three patterns: 0, 0, 0."* A literal substring search for `def main` hits 6 declarations in 3 family files (`SubexpQuarticSelectorAdapter.lean:109 def mainProgram`, `SubexpSetupProcedure.lean:10,30,48 def mainExtraStacks/mainStack/mainProcedure`, `SubexpMainSparsify.lean:333,351`); the count is 0 only with a word boundary. The conclusion (no entry point) is right; the pattern should be stated.

**R10 (B, minor).** p. 61, B.4: *"The 14,034 lines with decide ..."*. My recount with a different comment-stripper gives 14,078. Method-dependent; no definition of "line with decide" beyond naming `closure.py`.

## C — sentences that read as overclaims beyond the Lean statement

**R11 (C).** Title (p. 1): *"...the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size."* versus p. 4, §1.3, Not claimed 1: *"No running time of the verified machine, in either direction. ... the title compares two guarantees."* and p. 36, §7.1: *"the verified algorithm's actual running time on any formula is unknown, in both directions: nothing says it is slow"*. Taken literally, "the algorithm is slower" is a running-time claim the body expressly disowns; the body makes it true only through §2.5's gloss. The title is fixed and not to be altered; this is filed so the owner knows the paper's own non-claim list contradicts the title's literal wording and the reconciliation rests entirely on §2.5 and §7.4.

## D — out of scope

None found. The only graph vocabulary (independent sets, colourings, perfect matchings, p. 15; "circuits, graphs, maps", p. 14) is the manuscript's own quoted corollary objects; `SubexpPathwidth` appears only as the directory name; no sentence mentions treewidth, pathwidth, width parameters, crossovers or MOSP. The quoted §1.1.3 list is elided as "[...]".

## E — title altered

None. Page 1 carries the title with the ellipsis typeset as three spaced dots and 2^n as a superscript (recorded as S2); `pdfinfo` Title is exactly `Exponential time hypothesis is false... but the verified algorithm is slower than trying all 2^n assignments at every practical size.`; there are no running headers; `paper3/lean/README.md` quotes the title verbatim.

## F — plain-English stalls and internal inconsistencies

**R12 (F).** Table 2.1 (p. 8) gives rho(50) = **50.14**; B.6 (p. 62) says *"up to the rounding of rho(50) = 50.13"*. rho(50) = 50.1351. Also "equal to 1 up to rounding" is generous: 2^(rho(50)−50) = 1.098.

**R13 (F).** Table 7.1 (p. 37), column "sat", caption *"the number of satisfiable instances among those CaDiCaL finished"*; rows n = 400 and n = 500 read **"10/20"** and **"8/20"** while only 10 and 8 instances finished (the CSV confirms 10/10 and 8/8). Under the caption's definition the cells should read 10/10 and 8/8, or the caption should say "of the twenty".

**R14 (F).** p. 41, §8.1: *"our file has no sorry and no axiom declaration anywhere (the same script counts them: zero and zero in each of our two files)"*. `sorry` occurs in `PrimitiveMachine.lean` line 10 (header comment) and `axiom` in `Frame.lean` lines 13 and 83 (comments). `rediff.log` says "outside the header comment"; the paper drops the qualifier.

**R15 (F).** Terms used before their gloss: `lean4export` and `nanoda_lib` in Table 4.2 rows (iv)/(v) and §4.8 (p. 25), glossed only in §5.3 (p. 27); `tmpfs` (p. 26) and `SHA-256` (pp. 27, 28, 44) never glossed; the notation `m = ⌊4.26 n⌉` (mixed brackets, pp. 36–38) unexplained (the data show rounding to nearest: 85 at n = 20). Everything else the brief lists is glossed at or before first use.

